WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:03.169
NARRATOR: Ambushed on a
desolate road at night,

00:00:03.236 --> 00:00:06.072
Andrew repels a coyote attack.

00:00:06.139 --> 00:00:10.243
But the coyote isn't alone.

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:14.647
As soon as I hit the
initial coyote on the head

00:00:14.714 --> 00:00:17.684
with the flashlight, that's
when the other two, you know,

00:00:17.751 --> 00:00:20.854
saw the aggression from
me and started to attack.

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:23.523
It was overwhelming.

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:26.393
I figured that hitting the first
one, it would have been done.

00:00:26.459 --> 00:00:27.360
But it wasn't.

00:00:27.427 --> 00:00:30.463
All it did was
just make it worse.

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:32.065
NARRATOR: Andrew
is now under attack

00:00:32.132 --> 00:00:35.335
by three coyotes, a pack.

00:00:35.402 --> 00:00:40.340
This is a species that has
evolved this kind of pattern,

00:00:40.407 --> 00:00:44.911
this behavioral pattern of
increased hunting success

00:00:44.978 --> 00:00:46.312
when you're in a pack.

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:50.417
If coyotes are in a pack,
they have the ability

00:00:50.483 --> 00:00:53.119
to bring down quite
large prey, including

00:00:53.186 --> 00:00:55.889
a herd animals, like
white-tailed deer and even

00:00:55.955 --> 00:00:57.123
humans.

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:59.359
But overall, given
the number of humans

00:00:59.426 --> 00:01:01.294
and given the number
of coyotes, it's

00:01:01.361 --> 00:01:03.329
still a very rare phenomenon.

00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:06.933
Why would a group
of coyotes attack

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.770
a human is a bit of a mystery.

00:01:10.837 --> 00:01:12.539
At that point,
it was just mayhem.

00:01:12.605 --> 00:01:15.241
And mind that it's 3, 4
o'clock in the morning.

00:01:15.308 --> 00:01:17.510
And the only thing
I really see is

00:01:17.577 --> 00:01:20.647
a general outline of the
animal and just the shimmer

00:01:20.713 --> 00:01:21.781
of the light of the ice.

00:01:21.848 --> 00:01:24.751
Humans, we are
not as well-equipped

00:01:24.818 --> 00:01:26.719
as coyotes are at night.

00:01:26.786 --> 00:01:28.188
They can sleep better.

00:01:28.254 --> 00:01:31.858
They have ice shine, this
reflective iridescent set

00:01:31.925 --> 00:01:35.462
of cells in the back of the
retina that serve to capture

00:01:35.528 --> 00:01:39.566
light and then bounce that
light back out, so that then

00:01:39.632 --> 00:01:42.035
the optic nerve,
ultimately, is picking

00:01:42.101 --> 00:01:44.304
up on the light information.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:45.605
So they see a lot better.

00:01:45.672 --> 00:01:47.240
ANDREW DICKEHAGE: All
I could think about

00:01:47.307 --> 00:01:48.541
was I needed both hands.

00:01:48.608 --> 00:01:50.343
I couldn't do anything
with the flashlight.

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:53.046
Imagine, like, having three or
four people just in your face.

00:01:53.112 --> 00:01:54.180
So I dropped the flashlight.

00:01:54.247 --> 00:01:55.949
And I just put my hands up.

00:01:56.015 --> 00:01:57.517
At first, I wasn't
even actually trying

00:01:57.584 --> 00:02:00.320
to hurt them because I know
that wildlife is territorial.

00:02:00.386 --> 00:02:02.822
And you know, I'm just trying to
get away from where it started.

00:02:02.889 --> 00:02:05.992
And they're essentially just
kicking up off of my waistline,

00:02:06.059 --> 00:02:07.494
trying to get up and higher.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:13.299
By far and away, the
most vulnerable aspect

00:02:13.366 --> 00:02:17.637
of any vertebrate is
the neck and the head.

00:02:17.704 --> 00:02:19.138
That's where the brain is.

00:02:19.205 --> 00:02:20.740
That's where eyes.

00:02:20.807 --> 00:02:25.445
That's where the very anatomy of
a respiration is taking place.

00:02:25.512 --> 00:02:28.948
So ultimately, in bringing
down a prey species,

00:02:29.015 --> 00:02:31.584
they want to go for
that region of the body.

00:02:31.651 --> 00:02:33.286
The only thing that
I really could do

00:02:33.353 --> 00:02:36.789
was keep myself from
falling down and making sure

00:02:36.856 --> 00:02:38.057
that they didn't get behind me.

00:02:38.124 --> 00:02:40.159
After a while, it
really started to hurt.

00:02:40.226 --> 00:02:41.661
There were gashes on my arms.

00:02:41.728 --> 00:02:44.497
They were essentially just
opening up the wounds more.

00:02:44.564 --> 00:02:47.100
One tactic that
coyotes [inaudible]

00:02:47.166 --> 00:02:48.434
to get lots of little bites.

00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:51.037
And the prey starts
bleeding out.

00:02:51.104 --> 00:02:52.739
Really, all I could
process at that moment

00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:55.575
was keep one foot in the dirt
and one foot on the pavement.

00:02:55.642 --> 00:02:57.710
I don't want to lose
any kind of advantage

00:02:57.777 --> 00:02:59.012
that I had in the situation.

00:02:59.078 --> 00:03:00.613
But when the adrenaline
started to wear off,

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:01.381
I did start to get tired.

00:03:01.447 --> 00:03:02.815
And the pain started to set in.

00:03:02.882 --> 00:03:04.217
It was starting to
get to the point

00:03:04.284 --> 00:03:06.085
where I felt like I had no
control of the situation

00:03:06.152 --> 00:03:08.421
and that they were starting
to get the upper hand.

00:03:11.157 --> 00:03:14.627
[music playing]

